#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055933 :tutorial/content "When simplifying numerical expressions, it is generally preferable to avoid having radicals in the denominator. In cases where we wish to remove a radical from a denominator, we must **rationalize the denominator**.\n\nIf the denominator of an expression consists of two terms and includes one or more radicals, we rationalize it by multiplying both the numerator and the denominator by the **conjugate** of the denominator. \n\nTo illustrate, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{1+\\sqrt{2}}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the conjugate of the denominator $1+\\sqrt{2},$ we *flip the sign* between the two terms. So, the conjugate of the denominator is \n\n$$\n1\\,{\\color{blue}{-}}\\,\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we start by multiplying both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $1-\\sqrt{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{1+\\sqrt{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1-\\sqrt{2}}{1-\\sqrt{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\cdot (1-\\sqrt{2})}{(1+\\sqrt{2})(1-\\sqrt{2})} &  \\\\ \\frac{1-\\sqrt{2}}{(1+\\sqrt{2})(1-\\sqrt{2})} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n(a+b)(a-b) = a^2 - b^2\n$$\n\nApplying the difference of squares formula with $a=1, b=\\sqrt 2$ to simplify the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-\\sqrt{2}}{(1+\\sqrt{2})(1-\\sqrt{2})} & = \\\\ \\frac{1-\\sqrt{2}}{{1}^{2}-(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1-\\sqrt{2}}{1-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{1-\\sqrt{2}}{-1} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2b34f4d4-8931-5c24-8872-dfa9420a1a87" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13463 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
